---
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import pcre242License from '@/lib/regex-lab/wasm/PCRE2-10.42-LICENCE.txt?url';
import pcre243License from '@/lib/regex-lab/wasm/PCRE2-10.43-LICENCE.txt?url';
import pcre249License from '@/lib/regex-lab/wasm/PCRE2-10.49-LICENCE.txt?url';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'STRling Lab', href: '/lab/' },
  { label: 'Lab guide', href: '/lab/guide/' },
];
const expectations = [
  '{"kind":"boolean","value":false}',
  '{"kind":"matches","count":2,"captures":[{"match":1,"key":"name","value":"alpha"}]}',
  '{"kind":"matches","count":0}',
  '{"kind":"replacement","value":"result"}',
  '{"kind":"split","value":["first",null,"last"]}',
  '{"kind":"error","value":"compile-rejection"}',
].join('\n');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lab guide"
  description="How to use the Labs, choose a runtime, inspect results, and save sessions."
  pathname="/lab/guide/"
  {crumbs}
>
  <article class="container reading section">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Lab guide</h1>
    <p>
      Use <a href="/regex/lab/">RegEx Lab</a> to run raw patterns. Use <a
        href="/lab/">STRling Lab</a
      > to compile Semantic STRling or Simply builder JSON and inspect the resulting
      target artifact.
    </p>

    <h2>Run a pattern</h2>
    <p>
      Enter pattern source without host-language quotes or regex literal
      delimiters, then set flags separately. Choose an operation and enter text
      in Subject. Run with the button or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> / <kbd>Command</kbd> + <kbd
        >Enter</kbd
      >. Live mode runs after edits. Stop terminates the current worker and
      pauses Live mode.
    </p>
    <p>
      Search returns a boolean. Full match requires the runtime to match the
      entire subject. Find first and Find all return match and capture records.
      Select a result to locate its subject range. Ranges are half-open and use
      the engine's native unit. The editor also shows converted UTF-16 offsets
      for valid Unicode boundaries.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        JavaScript uses the browser's RegExp implementation and UTF-16 offsets.
        Scan and Replace all set global mode. Split inserts captured delimiters
        and uses native String.split behavior.
      </li>
      <li>
        PCRE2 runs in UTF-8 mode and reports byte offsets. The Lab's <code
          >p</code
        > flag enables Unicode properties. Split uses reported delimiters and includes
        captured fields. Native replacement uses PCRE2's basic substitution syntax.
      </li>
      <li>
        Python uses CPython's <code>re</code> in WebAssembly and reports Unicode code-point
        offsets. Operations use the corresponding text APIs, including <code
          >fullmatch</code
        >, <code>finditer</code>, <code>sub</code>, and <code>split</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The Python bytes provider encodes the subject and replacement as UTF-8 and
      displays observed bytes in hexadecimal. Some byte ranges have no
      equivalent selection in a Unicode text editor.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pick Literal text to insert a replacement verbatim. Choose Runtime
      template to use the engine's syntax, such as JavaScript <code
        >$&lt;name&gt;</code
      >, Python <code>\g&lt;name&gt;</code>, or PCRE2 <code
        >$&#123;name&#125;</code
      >. The JSON result records the runtime and operation. In Results, open
      Extract matches or captures to download one value per match as JSON. An
      empty capture remains an empty string and a nonparticipating capture is
      recorded as null.
    </p>

    <h2>Compile STRling source</h2>
    <p>
      Choose Semantic STRling 1.0 or Simply builder JSON. The Simply editor
      accepts protocol 1.0 or 1.1 builder objects. The Lab supplies the compile
      projection and exact selected target. Open Compiler for diagnostics,
      Semantic IR, analysis, and portability decisions supplied by STRling
      4.0.3. Artifact shows the emitted text, flags, applicable options, and
      source mappings. Eligible ECMAScript, PCRE2, and Python 3.11 artifacts run
      directly in STRling Lab. Open in RegEx Lab to explicitly select a
      different environment while preserving the original artifact.
    </p>
    <p>
      Format JSON indents Simply builder input. In a build with the canonical
      compiler tools, Format STRling applies the language formatter or shows
      diagnostics linked to the source. Open Semantic tools to convert the
      current program to Semantic STRling or Simply 1.0. An exact result
      includes reconstruction evidence and can be opened in the editor. Partial
      results show what needs review, and unsupported results explain why there
      is no output.
    </p>
    <p>
      Explain current subject evaluates a bounded canonical search of the
      current subject, with either the selected target profile or target-neutral
      semantics. The result distinguishes matched, no match, unknown, and
      unavailable, and labels the confidence of each finding. Use Results to see
      a native runtime observation. The explanation runs locally and its
      evidence contains subject identity and locations without the subject text.
      The Semantic tools panel shows availability for the current build.
    </p>

    <h2>Inspect structure and step through execution</h2>
    <p>
      Structure parses ECMAScript syntax and links constructs to pattern ranges.
      Debugger records PCRE2 interpreter callouts for a search. The instrumented
      run disables start optimizations and automatic possessification. It
      displays positions and capture state exposed by PCRE2. JavaScript and
      Python steps are unavailable.
    </p>

    <h2 id="test-expectations">Test expectations</h2>
    <p>
      Add a named test and supply its subject, operation, and group. Add from
      result fills an expectation using the last observation. Expected JSON
      examples
    </p>
    <pre><code>{expectations}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Use a numbered or named capture key. A null capture value expects a
      defined group that did not participate. For error expectations, categories
      include <code>compile-rejection</code>, <code>runtime-error</code>, <code
        >resource-terminated</code
      >, <code>unsupported-operation</code>, and <code
        >infrastructure-failure</code
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Compare and measure</h2>
    <p>
      Save a revision before changing a pattern, then use Compare to run both
      revisions on the current datasets and tests. RegEx Lab can also run the
      same raw pattern and options in a second runtime. The results include the
      witness input and both observations. Timing runs a bounded experiment with
      a warmup and shows worker compile time, execution time, and end-to-end
      latency where available.
    </p>

    <h2>Save and share</h2>
    <p>
      Save revision stores a snapshot in this browser. Use Sessions to restore,
      duplicate, or delete saved work. Settings links carry selections such as
      runtime, target, and operation. The source and subject are blank when the
      recipient opens one. To share your source, download a session file. Sample
      data, tests, recorded outputs, and original STRling source for a
      transferred artifact each require opt-in. Tests and recorded outputs may
      contain subject text even if the sample data box is cleared. Imported or
      shared material waits for an explicit action before executing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Restoring a revision lets you inspect its recorded outputs. Run creates a
      separate observation using the current runtime and inputs. Operations
      start from fresh runtime state. Saved revisions do not resume a matching
      cursor or an in-progress scan.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Labs use local workers and load engine assets from this site. Python
      needs a separate runtime download on first use. Each observation describes
      a particular input and environment. The <a href="/regex/compatibility/"
        >RegEx Compatibility Check</a
      > tracks certified evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Runtime source and notices are available for <a
        href={pcre242License}
        download>PCRE2 10.42</a
      >, <a href={pcre243License} download>PCRE2 10.43</a>, <a
        href={pcre249License}
        download>PCRE2 10.49</a
      >, and <a href="https://github.com/pyodide/pyodide">Pyodide</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
